course: everyday
lesson: 228
title: Safe and kind in parks and playgrounds
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A park is shared by people, dogs and wild animals, so the rules there protect all of them: keep to the paths where signs ask, leave wild animals alone, and give way to people walking.
explain: Most playground injuries come from falls and from using equipment the wrong way: climbing up slides, standing on swings, jumping from high places. Cords and scarves can also catch on equipment and strangle a child.
explain: If you find something dangerous, like broken glass or a needle, or someone gets badly hurt, you do not touch it or move them. You move away and get a grown-up at once.
explain: Example: in July a girl in Phoenix, Arizona, touches the metal slide before sliding down and finds it is hot enough to burn. She chooses the shady swings instead.
fact: «Keep off the grass» signs mean the grass may be newly seeded or resting, so walk on the paths.
fact: Bread fills ducks up without feeding them, makes them sick and dirties the water, so give only a little duck food, peas or oats, or nothing.
fact: Do not feed or touch squirrels and other wild animals, because they can bite and people food is bad for them.
fact: Fresh paint sticks to clothes, so read «wet paint» signs and choose another bench.
fact: Before you pat a stranger's dog, ask the owner, then let the dog sniff your hand before you stroke its side.
fact: Go home before dark or stay with your grown-up, because in the dark it is harder to see dangers and to be seen.
fact: On a shared path, ride slowly, keep to one side, ring your bell and give way to people walking.
fact: Metal and plastic in strong sun can burn skin, so touch a slide with your hand before sliding.
fact: Go up the ladder and down the slide, one at a time, so nobody crashes.
fact: Sit in the middle of a swing, hold on with both hands and stop it before getting off, because jumping off is a common cause of broken arms.
fact: Take off scarves, helmets and clothes with cords before playing on equipment, because they can catch and strangle.
fact: Never touch broken glass or needles; move away and tell a grown-up, because needles can carry dangerous illnesses.
fact: Do not move a child who has fallen and does not get up; stay close and call a grown-up at once.
fact: Keep off icy equipment, because ice is very slippery and falls on it are hard.
exercise: A sign in the park says «Keep off the grass». Why is it there? | The grass may be newly seeded or resting | Dogs are buried under the grass | The grass belongs to the café and only customers may use it | Walking on grass is bad for shoes
exercise: What should you feed the ducks at the pond? | A little duck food, peas or oats, or nothing | Plenty of white bread, as much as they will eat | Crisps and biscuits | Whatever you have left from lunch
exercise: Why is bread bad for ducks? | It fills them up without feeding them and dirties the water | Ducks cannot swallow anything soft | Bread makes their feathers fall out | Bread is fine for ducks; it is only cake and sweets that are bad
exercise: A squirrel runs right up to you as if it wants food. What should you do? | Watch it from a distance without feeding or touching it | Hold out a nut on your hand | Pick it up gently to stroke it | Chase it so it learns not to beg
exercise: A bench has a «wet paint» sign on it. What should you do? | Choose another bench | Touch it to check if it is really wet | Sit on it with a coat underneath | Sit on it, since signs are often old
exercise: You want to stroke a dog in the park. What is the first step? | Ask the owner if you may | Hug the dog around the neck | Reach out to pat its head quickly | Call the dog over to you to play
exercise: It is getting dark and your friends want to stay in the park. What should you do? | Go home or stay with your grown-up | Stay, as the park is safer at night | Stay alone and use your phone light | Hide in the bushes to play a hiding game
exercise: You are cycling on a path shared with walkers. What is right? | Ride slowly, ring your bell and give way to walkers | Ride fast so that you are past them all quickly and safely | Expect walkers to move out of your way | Ride in the middle so everyone sees you
exercise: Why should you touch a slide before sliding on a hot summer day? | Metal and plastic in strong sun can burn skin | Hot slides are much faster and more fun to go down | To see whether it is wet from rain | To leave your mark on it
exercise: Your friend climbs up the slide while others are coming down. Why is it a problem? | They can crash and both get hurt | It wears out the slide | It makes the slide too slippery | It is only a problem if a teacher sees
exercise: What is the safe way to use a swing? | Sit in the middle, hold on with both hands and stop before getting off | Stand on the seat to go higher | Jump off at the highest point | Twist the chains and spin as fast as you can
exercise: Why should you take off your scarf before using the climbing frame? | It can catch on the equipment and strangle you | It will get dirty and need washing again by your parents | It makes you climb too slowly | Scarves are not allowed in parks
exercise: You find a needle in the sandpit. What should you do? | Not touch it, move away and tell a grown-up | Pick it up carefully and throw it in the bin | Cover it with sand so nobody sees it | Show it to your friends first
exercise: A boy falls off the climbing frame and lies still. What should you do? | Not move him; stay close and call a grown-up at once | Help him stand up quickly | Drag him into the shade | Leave him to rest for a while and keep on playing nearby so he isn't alone
exercise: Your friend dares you to jump from the top of the climbing frame. What is the best answer? | Say no and climb down | Jump, because a dare is a promise | Jump, but land on your hands | Ask a younger child to jump first
exercise: The playground equipment is covered in ice. What should you do? | Keep off it until it thaws | Use it carefully with gloves on | Slide on it, since ice makes it faster | Brush off a little ice and climb
test: A girl in Brisbane touches a slide that has been in the midday sun and it feels very hot. What should she do? | Not slide on it and play somewhere shady | Slide quickly so the heat does not hurt | Pour her drink on it and slide straight away | Slide on it in shorts to cool down
test: A boy in Vermont wants to go on the climbing frame in a hoodie with long cords. What is the danger? | The cords could catch and choke him | He will be far too warm climbing in a hoodie | The hoodie will get torn | He will not be able to see well
test: Ella sees a man feeding a whole loaf of bread to the ducks. Which is true? | Too much bread can make ducks sick and dirty the pond | Ducks need lots of bread in winter to keep themselves warm | It is fine, ducks only eat what they need | Bread is better for ducks than peas
test: A small child is crying because she cut her hand on a broken bottle in the sandpit. What should you do? | Tell a grown-up and keep others away from the glass | Pick up all the pieces of glass yourself to help clear it | Bury the glass deeper in the sand | Tell her to stop crying and play elsewhere
test: Why do so many arms get broken on swings? | Children jump off or stand up on them | Swings are made of weak metal that snaps easily | Swings go far too slowly for real safety | Children hold on tight with both hands
test: A boy riding his scooter through the park comes up behind a family walking slowly. What should he do? | Slow down, ring his bell and pass carefully | Shout loudly so that they jump aside out of his way | Squeeze between them at full speed | Ride on the grass behind them
test: Two children are playing chase when one falls off a high platform and says his neck hurts. Why must they not lift him? | Moving someone with a hurt neck can make it worse | He will be far too heavy for two children to carry safely | His clothes will get dirty | It is fine as long as they are gentle
test: A girl wants to stroke a big dog on a lead. The owner says «Yes, but slowly». What should she do next? | Let the dog sniff her hand, then stroke its side | Hug the dog's head straight away | Bend down and stare deep into its eyes so it trusts her | Run up to it so it gets excited
test: A squirrel in a London park takes a crisp from a boy's hand. Why is that a bad idea? | People food is bad for it and it might bite | Squirrels only eat crisps at night, so it will be hungry | It is good for squirrels to eat salty food | The park keepers will charge him
test: slide's been in the sun all day, can i go down it? | Touch it with your hand first to check it's not too hot | Yes, slides never get hot | Only in bare legs so it's faster | Pour sand on it first
test: gonna throw my old bread to the ducks yeah? | No, bread makes them sick, give a little duck food or peas | Yes, bread is their best food | Yes, the more bread the better | Only if it's mouldy bread
test: found a needle by the bench, can i pick it up n bin it | No, move away and tell a grown-up | Yes, if you're careful and hold the end | Yes, with a tissue | Kick it under the bench
test: wanna jump off the swing at the top, looks cool | No, stop the swing first, jumping off breaks arms | Yes, it's the best way off | Yes, if you land on grass | Only if your friend jumps too
explain: A boy in Wisconsin went down a slide with a drawstring hoodie on; the cord caught at the top and choked him, and an adult had to cut him free.
explain: A girl in Brisbane picked up a needle in the sandpit to put it in the bin; she pricked her finger and had to have blood tests and medicine for months.
fact: Each year many children break arms and legs falling from playground equipment, often from jumping or standing on it.
fact: A needle prick from a used needle can pass on serious illnesses, so needles are only picked up by trained adults.
fact: Grown-ups' talk you may overhear is private: «his dad's left» means the dad no longer lives with the family, so be kind and do not repeat it; and teasing that two friends are «married» or a couple is just teasing, which you can ignore.
test: Why are cords and hoodie strings dangerous on slides? | They can catch and choke a child | They make the slide much slower for everyone | They get dirty on the slide very quickly | They make the child slip off the side
test: kid fell off the top, hurt his neck, says he's fine now | Don't move him, get an adult | Help him stand up slowly if he says he's fine | Carry him home | Tell him to walk it off
test: mate wants to climb on top of the swing bar | No, it's too high, a fall can break bones | Yes, just hold on tight with both hands and you'll be fine | Yes, if you go together | Only if it's dry
test: did u hear a kid jumped off the swing at the top and broke both arms, both!! real lesson? | Stop the swing before getting off; jumping breaks arms | Jump only from halfway up | Land on your hands to be safe | Only big kids should jump
test: apparently feeding bread to ducks makes them literally explode, my brother said. what's probably blown up? | Explode; but bread really makes ducks sick | That ducks eat food | That bread fills them up | That ducks live in ponds
test: my mate said a kid in the park picked up a needle and now has some disease forever. should u spread it? | Just learn the lesson: never touch needles, tell an adult | Yes, scare everyone with the story | Yes, and say which kid it was | Yes, and add more details
exam: Identify the correct manner of getting off a swing. | The swing is brought to a stop before one dismounts | One jumps clear from the highest point of the swing's arc | One lets go with both hands at speed | One leans backwards until one falls
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises bread as food for ducks? | It fills them without nourishing them and makes them ill | It is the most nourishing food for ducks of every kind and age | It is harmless in any quantity | It improves the water of the pond
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice where a child has fallen and does not rise. | He is not moved; one remains close and calls an adult | He is lifted to his feet at once | He is carried home without delay | He is left alone to recover
exam: Ascertain the reason for which scarves and clothing with cords are removed before using playground equipment. | They may catch and cause strangulation | They become dirty on the equipment and must be laundered | They slow children down on the slides considerably | They are against the rules of schools only
test: older kids at the park pointing at a couple on the bench "ewww they're snogging" | The couple are kissing, which is their private business; ignore it and keep playing | Go and watch them | Throw things at them | Take photos and share them
test: big kid said "u n Maisie are on the swings again, are u married lol" | He is teasing that you are a couple; ignore it or say you are just friends | You must stop playing with Maisie | You are married now | Push the big kid off the swing
test: heard a mum at the playground say "his dad's left, poor lad" bout Theo | Theo's dad does not live with the family any more; be kind and play with Theo, do not tell him what you heard | Tease Theo about it | Ask Theo why his dad left | Tell all the kids
